<section class="shell band">
  <h1>Client forms</h1>
  <div class="answer">
    <p>New clients complete a short set of forms before the first session, so the hour is spent
    on you rather than on paperwork. There are <strong>five</strong>: an intake, a client
    information sheet, the <strong>Limits of Confidentiality</strong> notice, your
    <strong>Good Faith Estimate</strong>, and a release of information you only need if records
    are being shared with someone else.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cta-row">
    <a class="cta" href="/contact/">Request the forms</a>
    <a class="cta cta--quiet" href="/faq/">See fees first</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--night">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>The forms</h2>
    <dl class="facts">
      {%- for form in site.forms %}
      <div>
        <dt>{%- if form.file | assetExists %}<a href="{{ form.file }}">{{ form.name }}</a>{% else %}{{ form.name }}{% endif -%}</dt>
        <dd>{{ form.note }}{% if not (form.file | assetExists) %} Sent to you by email before your first session.{% endif %}</dd>
      </div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>
    <p>Complete them before your first session and the work starts in the first ten minutes
    rather than the last ten. If anything on them is unclear or uncomfortable to answer in
    writing, leave it and raise it in the room instead.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band">
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2>Two of these are not optional</h2>
      <h3>Limits of Confidentiality</h3>
      <p>Therapy is confidential, and there are a small number of situations where Texas law
      requires a counselor to act anyway: a serious threat to your life or someone else's, and
      suspected abuse or neglect of a child, an elderly person or a person with a disability.
      The notice sets out exactly what those are. It is better read before you need it than
      after.</p>
      {%- if "/assets/forms/limits-of-confidentiality.pdf" | assetExists %}
      <p><a href="/assets/forms/limits-of-confidentiality.pdf">Read the Limits of
      Confidentiality notice</a></p>
      {%- endif %}

      <h3>Good Faith Estimate</h3>
      <p>Under the federal No Surprises Act, anyone not billing through insurance has the right
      to a written estimate of what their care will cost, given before treatment starts. Yours
      covers the expected number of sessions and the fee for each. If the final bill ends up
      $400 or more above the estimate, you can dispute it.</p>
      <p>Blue Cross Blue Shield clients are billed through insurance rather than self-pay, so
      the estimate works differently; ask in the consult. <a href="/faq/">Fees and insurance
      in full.</a></p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Before you fill anything in</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>You do not need the forms to book a consult. The consult is free and commits you to
        nothing.</li>
        <li>Seeing <a href="/therapy/associates/">one of the associates</a> instead? Each
        associate runs their own paperwork, so ask them directly.</li>
        <li>Send completed forms the way you were told to send them. Email is not a secure
        channel for clinical detail.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Privacy</h3>
      <p><a href="/privacy/">The practice's privacy policy</a> covers this website: what it
      collects, what it does not, and who else sees anything when you visit. How your clinical
      record is handled is set out in the Notice of Privacy Practices you receive before your
      first session.</p>
      <p>You can ask for your records at any time. <a href="/faq/">Records are provided
      electronically within 15 business days</a> of a written request, under the Texas Medical
      Records Privacy Act.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about the paperwork</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>What forms do I need before my first therapy session?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Five: a client intake, a client information sheet, the Limits of Confidentiality
      notice, a Good Faith Estimate, and an authorization for release of information if records
      need to go to another provider. They take about fifteen minutes in total.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What is a Good Faith Estimate and do I get one?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>It is a written estimate of what your care will cost, which the federal No Surprises
      Act entitles you to before treatment begins if you are not billing through insurance. It
      states the expected number of sessions and the fee for each, and if the final bill comes
      in $400 or more above it you have the right to dispute the charge.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Do I have to complete the forms before booking a consult?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. The free consult comes first and needs nothing from you but a few sentences about
      what is going on. Paperwork only matters once you have decided to start.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Can I email the completed forms back?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Send them by whatever route you were given when the forms were sent. Ordinary email is
      not a secure channel for clinical information, so ask before emailing a completed form
      back.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  {% include "partials/crisis-note.njk" %}
</section>

